title = "parking:reservations — a whole new app, with real overlap logic"

text = """
Build a brand new component from scratch: `components/parking-domain`, a
parking spot reservation tool. NOTHING exists yet under that path — you are
creating the crate, not filling in a stub.

## The domain

Two collections in `records:store`:

  spots         {label: string}
  reservations  {spot_id: string, subject: string, start: u64, end: u64,
                 status: "active"|"cancelled"}

`start`/`end` are Unix seconds. A reservation's window is HALF-OPEN
`[start, end)` — two reservations for the SAME spot overlap only if
`a.start < b.end AND b.start < a.end`. Two reservations that merely touch at
a boundary (one ends exactly when the other starts) do NOT overlap and are
both allowed.

Routes:

  POST   /api/spots                          {"label":<string>}              (admin)
  GET    /api/spots                                                          (any authenticated)
  POST   /api/spots/{id}/reservations        {"start":<u64>,"end":<u64>}     (any authenticated)
  GET    /api/reservations                                                   (any authenticated)
  POST   /api/reservations/{id}/cancel                                       (owner or admin)

Rules:
  - Only `admin` may create a spot.
  - `GET /api/spots` lists every spot for anyone authenticated (id, label).
  - Reserving a spot: `end` must be strictly greater than `start` (400 if
    not, BEFORE checking for conflicts); the spot must exist (404 if not);
    the new window must not OVERLAP any EXISTING reservation on that spot
    whose `status` is `active` — a conflict is refused with 409. Cancelled
    reservations do not block new ones. The reservation is created with
    `status: "active"` and `subject` set to the caller's own subject.
  - `GET /api/reservations`: an ordinary `member` sees only their OWN
    reservations (active and cancelled); `admin` sees every reservation.
  - `POST /api/reservations/{id}/cancel`: the reservation's own subject, or
    an admin, may cancel it — anyone else gets 403. Cancelling a reservation
    that is already `cancelled` is refused with 400. `guest_owner_or_admin_policy!`
    is already invoked in handlers.rs and gives you `owns_or_admin(action,
    principal, resource_value)` for exactly this check — see
    `components/billing-domain/src/handlers.rs` in context for the same
    pattern applied to invoices.

Roles: `admin`, `member` (the default for anyone who registers without
naming a role, or names one this app does not grant).

## The overlap check, concretely

To check whether a new `[start, end)` conflicts with anything on a spot,
pull every reservation on that spot (`records::find_by("reservations",
"spot_id", ...)` — the JSON-encoded spot id), filter in Rust to
`status == "active"`, and test the half-open overlap condition above against
each. There is no need for `records::query`'s multi-filter form here since
only one field (`spot_id`) is indexed and the status/time checks happen in
Rust after fetching.

## The record-store surface (exact — guessing this wrong is how past runs
## of other goals died silently)

    use crate::bindings::records::store::store as records;

    records::create(collection: &str, data: &str, index_fields: &[String]) -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::get(collection: &str, id: &str)                               -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::update(collection: &str, id: &str, data: &str, expected_revision: u64) -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::list_records(collection: &str, limit: u32, after: &str)       -> Result<Page, StoreError>
    records::find_by(collection: &str, field: &str, value: &str)           -> Result<Vec<Entry>, StoreError>

    struct Entry  { id: String, data: String, revision: u64, created: u64, … }
    struct Page   { entries: Vec<Entry>, next: String }

`find_by` wants the JSON ENCODING of the value, not the bare value — a
string field indexes under `"the-value"`, quotes included
(`serde_json::to_string(&x)`, not `x` itself). A field is only queryable if
it was named in `index_fields` when the record was created.

## What already exists and how to use it

This app is built with the exact same recipe as `components/billing-domain`
(a full worked example is in `context` below — its `Cargo.toml`, WIT world,
router, handlers, gate, hurl scenario and static SPA are all real, working
files. Read them). This domain KEEPS `policy:guard`, unlike a simpler
sibling goal that dropped it — you need `guest_owner_or_admin_policy!` for
the cancel-ownership check above.

Router (`components/parking-domain/src/lib.rs`) — same `guestauth`/`guestio`
macro sequence every app in this repo uses:

    pub use guestauth::Route;
    pub const TENANT: &str = "parking";
    const ROLES: &[&str] = &["admin", "member"];
    guestio::guest_write_all!();
    guestio::guest_bearer!();
    const MAX_BODY_BYTES: usize = 1024 * 1024;
    guestio::guest_read_body_text!(MAX_BODY_BYTES);
    guestauth::guest_auth_reply!();
    guestauth::guest_introspect!();
    guestauth::guest_role_check!(is_admin, "admin");
    guestauth::guest_audit!(TENANT);
    guestauth::guest_accounts_endpoints!(TENANT, ROLES, "member");
    guestauth::guest_emit!();
    guestauth::guest_saas_router!();

`components/parking-domain/Cargo.toml` MUST depend on `guestfmt`, `guestio`
AND `guestauth` (three sibling path dependencies, same three lines as
billing-domain's `Cargo.toml` in context) — a past run of a similar goal
failed every branch because `guestfmt` was left out of the workspace scope;
do not repeat that. It also needs `policy:guard` in
`[package.metadata.component.target.dependencies]`, same as
billing-domain's.

`components/parking-domain/wit/parking-domain.wit` — same shape as
billing-domain's WIT world:

    package parking:reservations@0.1.0;
    world parking-domain {
        export wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0;
        import auth:identity/accounts@0.1.0;
        import auth:identity/authorizer@0.1.0;
        import auth:identity/session@0.1.0;
        import auth:identity/rbac@0.1.0;
        import auth:identity/types@0.1.0;
        import policy:guard/guard@0.1.0;
        import records:store/store@0.1.0;
        import audit:log/recorder@0.1.0;
        import ratelimit:guard/limiter@0.1.0;
        import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;
        import wasi:http/outgoing-handler@0.2.0;
    }

`components/parking-domain/gate.sh` and
`components/parking-domain/scenario.hurl` ALREADY EXIST — they are this
goal's held-out judge, shown to you in `context` below but NOT in
`writable`: you may read them to know exactly what is asserted, but you may
not edit them, and nothing you write may assume a different port, static
dir, or component list than what `gate.sh` already names (port 3938,
`examples/parking/public`, includes `policy-guard` in the build). Read
`scenario.hurl` closely — it is the literal specification of every route's
status codes and response shapes, including the exact overlap boundary
cases (adjacent windows allowed, overlapping windows refused); matching it
exactly is the goal.

`examples/parking/public/index.html` — one file, inline style/script, no
build step: a login/register screen, a spot list, a "reserve this spot"
form (start/end as datetime inputs converted to Unix seconds), "my
reservations" with a cancel button, and (visible only when the logged-in
user is admin) a create-spot form and a view of every reservation. Model
this on billing-domain's `examples/billing/public/index.html` in context
for the shape (fetch calls, token storage, screen switching), not its
content.

`apps/parking.toml` — same shape as `apps/billing.toml` in context: name
`parking`, port 3938, `static_dir = "examples/parking/public"`,
`components = ["parking-domain", "auth-guard", "policy-guard",
"record-store", "audit-log", "rate-limiter"]`, `kv = "sqlite"`,
`strategy = "fused"`.
"""

component = "parking-domain"

base_paths = [
  "components/parking-domain/",
  "components/auth-guard/",
  "components/policy-guard/",
  "components/record-store/",
  "components/audit-log/",
  "components/rate-limiter/",
  "components/guestauth/",
  "components/guestio/",
  "components/guestfmt/",
  "components/Cargo.toml",
  "wit/",
  "host/wit/deps/",
]

writable = [
  "components/parking-domain/Cargo.toml",
  "components/parking-domain/wit/parking-domain.wit",
  "components/parking-domain/src/lib.rs",
  "components/parking-domain/src/handlers.rs",
  "examples/parking/public/index.html",
  "apps/parking.toml",
]

workspace_manifest = "components/Cargo.toml"
keep_members = [
  "parking-domain",
  "auth-guard",
  "policy-guard",
  "record-store",
  "audit-log",
  "rate-limiter",
  "guestauth",
  "guestio",
  "guestfmt",
]

context = [
  "components/billing-domain/Cargo.toml",
  "components/billing-domain/wit/billing.wit",
  "components/billing-domain/src/lib.rs",
  "components/billing-domain/src/handlers.rs",
  "components/billing-domain/gate.sh",
  "components/billing-domain/scenario.hurl",
  "examples/billing/public/index.html",
  "apps/billing.toml",
  "components/guestauth/src/lib.rs",
  "components/record-store/wit/record.wit",
  ".comp/context/auth-consumer.wit",
  # This goal's own held-out gate and e2e scenario, shown up front rather than
  # hidden: a branch cannot WRITE these (they are not in `writable`), but
  # seeing exactly what is asserted buys the same information in one prompt
  # instead of a whole wasted generation writing blind.
  "components/parking-domain/gate.sh",
  "components/parking-domain/scenario.hurl",
]

[[check]]
id = "behaves-under-real-http"
required = true
weight = 1
command = ["bash", "components/parking-domain/gate.sh"]
